# Words that stay, each with its reason — `tools/spelling-check.mjs`.
#
# One per line: `path :: word :: why`. Checked BOTH directions: a declaration
# that no longer matches anything FAILS, so a scrub cannot leave this file
# quietly covering words that are gone. Every line prints on every run.
#
# THIS FILE IS EMPTY ON PURPOSE (3.23.13). It carried four declarations letting
# the 3.23.5 release note print the two spellings it had replaced, on the
# grounds that a note about a spelling change should show the before and the
# after. That was ruled the wrong way round: it is fine to say what changed,
# and the old spelling does not belong in the app to say it. The note now names
# the two controls and says each changed a letter.
#
# A word is declarable when it is a quotation, a proper name, or a spelling the
# text's whole job is to NAME. It is never declarable to avoid doing the work.
# The help has a better route for the same need: `<span data-was>` marks a
# control's former name per mention and is exempt without any declaration.

tools/gate-audit.mjs :: colours :: the spelling gate's own plant. It has to WRITE a British spelling to prove the gate catches one, so this is the third case the header names — a spelling whose whole job is to be named. It was an identity replace for three releases, both halves reading "Colors", because the sweep that took British spelling out of the app rewrote the fixture too; the audit reported the gate unverified, which is the only reason it was found.
